#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8787ecbb-bcde-5e1b-a5ed-9d23815522e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087109} {:answer/id #uuid "b54535fc-a8dc-5ada-b428-b490dbf1a18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087110} {:answer/id #uuid "c792fe58-82f5-5954-93e9-3119bea36ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} {:answer/id #uuid "6b67e556-e64d-523c-9321-f7fb1846ebc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087112} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9de150-76e4-5439-86c8-31f5b6d852a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos e^{\\arctan (x^{2} + \\ln x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c792fe58-82f5-5954-93e9-3119bea36ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{dy}{dx} + x = y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087111} :answer-field/id #uuid "7759e328-b997-576f-b938-3880d1aeb387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}]}
